    "product_id": "e0f542a7-b29c-4794-a46a-f89ac511ec68",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 120944\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n144 AM PST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Warm and mild weather will come to an end on Friday as cool,\n  unsettled weather returns to the region.\n\n* Additional precipitation arrives into the next work week with\n  only short breaks between systems.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nThursday looks to be the last day of our calm and mild weather \ntrend. Highs will peak in the 70s and 80s across the lower \nelevations of the region with light to moderate winds and a slow \nbuild of higher cloud cover. The pattern changes on Friday with c \nchances for snow and stronger winds pushing into the eastern Sierra \nby the late morning. A Winter Storm Warning is in effect for \nelevations above 7,000 feet in the Eastern Sierra for Friday into \nSaturday afternoon. Around a foot of new snow will be possible in \nthe Sierra Crest, and 5 to 10 inches of snow expected between 7,000 \nft and 9,500 ft. Rain chances and increasing winds spread across the \nregion into Friday afternoon. The strongest winds arrive before \nprecipitation, but breezy to gusty winds will last through much of \nthe day. A Wind Advisories are in effect Friday into early Saturday \nwith 20 to 30 mph sustained southerly winds and gusts to 50 mph. \nWinds across the region ease before rain fully exits the area, but \ncalmer and cooler conditions are expected for the rest of Saturday \nand through early Monday.\n\n\nMonday's precip arrives from the west as a low pressure builds into \nthe area early that morning and spreads across the region through \nthe day. As of the current forecast, snow in the upper elevations of \nthe Sierra from this system looks to stay relatively light as the \nfeed of moisture doesn't establish itself more consistently until the \nsystem moves more to the east. Rain becomes widespread across the \nrest of the CWA into the afternoon with light to moderate rain \nrates. This low pressure looks to slow over the region leading to \nrain chances lasting into early Wednesday. Rain rates look to stay \nlight but will add up over time. The break from rain looks to be \nshort lived as another system will be possible late in the Wednesday \nand offer scattered showers into the early weekend.\n\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...\nLight, generally southwest winds are expected through the overnight \nperiod under clear skies. Expect winds to become northeast after \n18Z, but remaining under 7 knots through the afternoon. Increasing \nhigh level clouds are expected late Thursday afternoon. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light and diurnal winds \nanticipated across much of the region through the TAF period, with a \nfew exceptions. KIFP will see localized areas of northerly winds \ngusting to around 20 knots from late morning through the afternoon. \nKBIH will also see stronger south winds sustained around 15 knots \nand gusts between 25 and 30 knots. Generally clear skies, with \nincreasing high clouds Thursday afternoon. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Gorelow\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
